# 6.1 Solids, liquids and gases · 7Cm.06, 7TWSm.01, 7SIC.01
# =====================================================================================================
READ["6.1"] = L(
    H("Why can you squash a ball but not a brick?",
      "A football is full of air and you can squeeze it. A water bottle filled to the top cannot be squashed at all. A brick does not change shape however hard you press.",
      "Why do solids, liquids and gases behave so differently? Write your idea first."),
    I("Three states of matter",
      "**Matter** is anything that has mass and takes up space. It exists in three **states**: solid, liquid and gas. Water can be ice, liquid water or water vapour.",
      table=[["Property", "Solid", "Liquid", "Gas"],
             ["shape", "fixed", "takes the shape of its container", "fills the whole container"], ["volume", "fixed", "fixed", "not fixed – spreads out"],
             ["can it flow?", "no", "yes", "yes"], ["can it be compressed (squashed)?", "no", "no (almost not)", "yes – easily"]],
      then=["Use: car brakes push a liquid along a pipe. A liquid cannot be squashed, so a push on the pedal reaches the brakes at once."]),
    I("The particle model",
      "Scientists explain these properties with the **particle model**: all matter is made of tiny **particles** that are always moving.",
      fig={"kind": "particles"},
      table=[["", "Solid", "Liquid", "Gas"],
             ["arrangement", "regular pattern, very close", "close and touching, no pattern", "far apart, random"],
             ["movement", "vibrate about fixed positions", "move around each other", "move fast in all directions"],
             ["forces between particles", "very strong", "strong", "almost none"]]),
    I("Predict, watch, explain: which one can be squashed?",
      "Three syringes are sealed at the end: one holds air, one water, one tightly packed sand. Each plunger is pushed hard.",
      then=["Only the **air** is squashed into a smaller volume. In a gas the particles are far apart with empty space between them, so they can be pushed closer.",
            "In the water and the sand the particles are already touching – there is no space to squash. That is why bicycle tyres are filled with air: it squashes to give a soft ride."]),
    Y("Which state?",
      grid("s7u06r11", "Which state of matter is described?", ["Solid", "Liquid", "Gas"],
           [("particles vibrate in fixed places", "Solid"), ("particles are far apart and move quickly", "Gas"), ("particles touch but slide past each other", "Liquid"),
            ("can be compressed easily", "Gas"), ("keeps its own shape", "Solid")],
           "Think about how close the particles are and how they move.", "Fixed places / own shape → solid; touching but sliding → liquid; far apart, squashable → gas.")),
    I("How the particle model developed",
      "Our ideas about particles were built up over more than 2000 years.",
      table=[["Who", "When", "Idea or evidence"],
             ["Democritus", "about 2400 years ago", "everything is made of tiny pieces called atoms – but he had no evidence"],
             ["John Dalton", "about 1800", "elements are made of atoms; atoms of one element are all alike"],
             ["Robert Brown", "1827", "saw pollen grains in water jiggling randomly under a microscope (**Brownian motion**)"],
             ["Albert Einstein", "1905", "explained it: fast, invisible water particles keep hitting the grains"],
             ["Jean Perrin", "1908", "experiments matched Einstein's predictions – strong evidence for particles"]],
      then=["Models change and improve when new evidence appears."]),
    I("How good is the particle model?",
      "Every model has strengths and limitations.",
      table=[["Strengths", "Limitations"],
             ["explains shape, flow and compression of solids, liquids and gases", "draws all particles as identical balls; real particles differ in size and shape"],
             ["explains melting, boiling and dissolving", "does not show the forces between particles"],
             ["simple to draw and use", "particles are not really coloured spheres, and there is no 'air' between them"]]),
    X("Gas particles are not bigger",
      "Some students think that particles get bigger when a solid turns into a gas.",
      "The particles stay the **same size**. In a gas they are **further apart** and move faster – that is why the gas takes up more space."),
    K("Key points",
      "Matter exists as **solid, liquid or gas**.",
      "Solid: fixed shape and volume. Liquid: fixed volume, flows. Gas: no fixed shape or volume, flows, can be compressed.",
      "Particle model: solid – close, regular, vibrate; liquid – close, random, move around; gas – far apart, fast, random.",
      "The model developed over time (Democritus, Dalton, Brown, Einstein, Perrin) and has strengths and limitations."),
    book=BK(
        [("matter", "anything that has mass and takes up space"), ("particle", "a tiny piece of matter"), ("compress", "squash into a smaller volume"),
         ("particle model", "the idea that all matter is made of tiny moving particles"), ("Brownian motion", "the random jiggling of tiny grains, caused by particles hitting them")],
        [], [],
        [Ex("Copy and complete the table for solids, liquids and gases: fixed shape? fixed volume? flows? can be compressed?",
            ans="solid: yes, yes, no, no; liquid: no, yes, yes, no; gas: no, no, yes, yes"),
         Ex("Use the particle model to explain why:", ("a liquid takes the shape of its container", "its particles can move around each other"),
            ("a gas can be compressed", "its particles are far apart, with empty space between them"), ("a solid keeps its shape", "its particles are held in fixed positions by strong forces")),
         Ex("Describe what Robert Brown saw and how Einstein explained it.", ans="Pollen grains in water jiggled randomly; invisible, fast-moving water particles keep hitting the grains."),
         Ex("Give one strength and one limitation of the particle model.", ans="Strength: explains the properties of the three states. Limitation: shows all particles as identical balls / does not show forces."),
         Ex("Why are car brakes filled with liquid and not air?", ans="A liquid cannot be compressed, so the push from the pedal is passed straight to the brakes; air would just squash.", think=True)],
        ["describe the properties of solids, liquids and gases", "explain them with the particle model", "describe how the particle model developed",
         "give strengths and limitations of the model"],
        look=["the particle arrangement table", "the squashing syringes"],
        note="Read it after the video: the three states, the particle model, how it developed and its limits (answers at the back)."),
)

